ournal">Ganetzky, B., G.A. Robertson, G.F. Wilson, M.C. Trudeau, and S.A. Titus. <year>1999</year>. The eag family of K+ channels in <italic>Drosophila</italic> and mammals. <source>Ann. NY Acad. Sci.</source> <volume>868</volume>:<fpage>356</fpage>&#x02013;369.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10414305</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><citation citation-type="journal">Gomez-Lagunas, F. <year>1997</year>. Shaker B K+ conductance in Na+ solutions lacking K+ ions: a remarkably stable non-conducting state produced by membrane depolarizations. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>499</volume>:<fpage>3</fpage>&#x02013;15.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9061636</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><citation citation-type="journal">Gomez-Lagunas, F., and C.M. Armstrong. <year>1994</year>. The relation between ion permeation and recovery from inactivation of ShakerB K+ channels. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>67</volume>:<fpage>1806</fpage>&#x02013;1815.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7858119</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib28"><citation citation-type="journal">Hamill, O.P., A. Marty, E. Neher, B. Sakmann, and F.J. Sigworth. <year>1981</year>. Improved patch-clamp techniques for high-resolution current recording from cells and cell-free membrane patches. <source>Pflugers Arch.</source> <volume>391</volume>:<fpage>85</fpage>&#x02013;100.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6270629</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib29"><citation citation-type="journal">Harris, R.E., H.P. Larsson, and E.Y. Isacoff. <year>1998</year>. A permanent ion binding site located between two gates of the Shaker K+ channel. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>74</volume>:<fpage>1808</fpage>&#x02013;1820.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9545043</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><citation citation-type="journal">Heginbotham, L., M. LeMasurier, L. Kolmakova-Partensky, and C. Miller. <year>1999</year>. Single streptomyces lividans K(+) channels. Functional asymmetries and sidedness of proton activation. <so